## 6. GEMINI API AND BACKEND SECURITY

Security is mandatory.

Implement the following protections:

1. Never place a private Gemini API key in frontend JavaScript.
2. Never commit secrets to source control.
3. Read secrets from environment variables.
4. Provide a .env.example file containing placeholder values only.
5. Add real secret files to .gitignore.
6. Route AI requests through the backend.
7. Validate request bodies on the server.
8. Apply sensible request-size limits.
9. Implement rate limiting on AI endpoints.
10. Apply request timeouts.
11. Handle provider failures and quota errors.
12. Return safe error messages without exposing stack traces or secrets.
13. Validate and sanitize user-provided strings where appropriate.
14. Prevent unsafe HTML rendering and cross-site scripting.
15. Use parameterized database operations or safe ODM queries.
16. Avoid unsafe dynamic evaluation.
17. Configure CORS for the intended deployment origin.
18. Do not trust client-supplied user IDs or authorization claims.
19. If authentication is implemented, verify identity and ownership on every protected operation.
20. Do not store passwords in plaintext.
21. Do not log API keys, credentials, or unnecessary sensitive user data.
22. Do not use client-side checks as the only security boundary.

If there is no authentication system, do not pretend that the app provides secure multi-user private storage.

For local demonstration, use a clearly identified demo mode with appropriate data limitations.

Add clear setup instructions explaining how to configure the Gemini API key securely.

